      { id: "dsa_m8", question: "What is the time complexity of inserting an element into a Binary Search Tree (BST) in the worst case (skewed tree)?", options: ["O(1)", "O(log N)", "O(N)", "O(N log N)"], answer: 2, explanation: "When insertions occur in sorted order, an unbalanced BST degenerates into a linked list with O(N) search and insertion time." },
      { id: "dsa_m9", question: "How does the Sliding Window algorithmic pattern achieve O(N) time when finding the longest substring with at most K distinct characters?", options: ["By expanding the right pointer and contracting the left pointer while maintaining a frequency map", "By generating all possible substrings and sorting them", "By using depth-first search on a suffix tree", "By hashing every sub-window independently"], answer: 0, explanation: "Both pointers move monotonically forward, visiting each index at most twice, resulting in O(N) time." },
      { id: "dsa_m10", question: "What is the difference between Separate Chaining and Open Addressing for resolving hash collisions?", options: ["Separate Chaining stores colliding elements in a linked list/tree at the bucket index; Open Addressing probes for the next open slot in the array", "Separate Chaining is only for strings; Open Addressing is for numbers", "Open Addressing allows infinite elements without resizing", "Separate Chaining requires O(N²) memory"], answer: 0, explanation: "Separate chaining handles collisions outside the main array via lists; open addressing finds alternative slots (linear/quadratic probing)." }
